Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Chrysalis Swallows the Coin-Slot
- Story & Context: On a freezing November night beneath the cracked skylights of the Starlight Galleria, Luka rammed a hot-soldered wire from his Hohner harmonica directly into the logic board of an open Space Invaders cabinet, filling the atrium with the acrid stench of roasted PCB board and ozone. Security guards shattered the glass perimeter doors with aluminum flashlights just as the drum machine kicked into a 160 BPM sprint, causing fifty squatting teenagers to swarm the barricades in total darkness. As the first amp caught fire and bathed the dead fountain in orange sparks, Luka spit blood from a cut lip and shrieked into the mic: "we don’t insert coins, we spit fire!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro close-up of a rusted metal arcade coin slot with a mechanical insect chrysalis splitting open inside the quarter aperture, illuminated by sizzling emerald cathode phosphor light and trailing smoking copper wires across cracked linoleum.

Track 2: Kendra’s Locust Flight
- Story & Context: Hidden in the storage crawlspace above a gutted Spencer Gifts, Kendra spent seventy-two uninterrupted hours sobbing over eviction notices while dousing her Juno-106 in isopropyl alcohol to scrub out industrial rust. The taste of copper reeds and warm metal lingered on her tongue as she forced her diatonic harp through an overdriven bass-cabinet, mimicking the deafening, suffocating roar of seventeen-year cicadas emerging from asphalt. When the mall's roof groaned under a sudden winter deluge, she recorded her breakdown in a single unedited take, shrieking the refrain: "hatch out of the chrome or die inside the cage!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric shot of an empty, rain-streaked mall concourse where hundreds of glowing green digital locusts burst out of shattered glass display cases, swirling upward into a dark skylight above a solitary synthesizer left on an escalator.

Track 3: "How Long Can We Breathe Solder?"
- Story & Context: By the spring of 1989, the city council finally ordered the Galleria demolished, yet Officer Miller testified that rhythmic industrial thumps and harmonica feedback still leaked from the padlocked Montgomery Ward entrance every midnight. The teenagers had already scattered along Interstate 80, but they had left a modified drum machine and tape-looped harp running on rigged solar cells nestled inside the HVAC ducting. The final artifact discovered before the wrecking ball swung was a charred piece of drywall spray-painted with the track's closing question: "the wasps own the mall now, boys!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Haunting wide shot of an abandoned, half-demolished shopping mall anchor store under a twilight sky, dead television sets buried in rubble, faint yellow caution tape fluttering in the wind, a lone spray-painted silhouette of a wasp on the remaining concrete wall.

